unit: inside-the-atom

post blog-the-smallest-thing
author: npc-hal
lesson: atoms-are-everywhere
title: The smallest thing you will ever meet
hook: You have never seen one, yet you are made of them. Let us look at how small they really are.
cover: atom
read: 1
references: ref-openstax-chem-2-1
references: ref-openstax-chem-ch1
references: ref-openstax-chem-ch2

p:
    Look at your hand. Now look at the air around it. Both are matter, and all matter is made of atoms.

p:
    Yes, even the air, which gives every impression of being nothing. That is why you can pump up a ball: the air inside it takes up space, because air is matter too. I have weighed several balls to be certain. The neighbours have asked me to stop.

quote:
    Small things are best looked at slowly. I still look at them every day.

h: How small is small?

p:
    An atom is about 10^-10 m across, one ten-billionth of a metre. The nucleus in its middle is about 100,000 times smaller still.

figure: atom
caption: An atom: far too small to see.

list:
    An atom: about 10^-10 m across
    Its nucleus: about 100,000 times smaller

fact:
    In the fifth century BC the Greek thinkers Leucippus and Democritus argued that all matter was made of small pieces they called atomos, a word for "indivisible". Seeing atoms took far longer.

p:
    So the next time you pick something up, remember that you are holding a huge crowd of atoms you can never see. Take a moment with that. I will wait.

next: Zoom in on an atom yourself => lesson atoms-are-everywhere

